{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xos8LTN4P8DxAwpVtTQWQT","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings:\n\n## Summary of pkg/sys Package Exports and Relevant Patterns\n\nBased on my exploration of the go-unix-utils repository, here are the key findings:\n\n### Key Exports from pkg/sys Package\n\n**File Location:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/pkg/sys/`\n\n#### 1. **FileInfo Struct** (fileinfo.go)\nA data structure holding extended file metadata unavailable through os.FileInfo:\n- `Mode` (os.FileMode)\n- `Size`, `Blocks`, `Blksize` (int64)\n- `Nlink`, `Uid`, `Gid`, `Rdev`, `Dev`, `Ino` (uint64 or uint32)\n- `ModTime`, `AccessTime`, `ChangeTime` (time.Time)\n- `Info` (os.FileInfo) - the underlying standard interface\n\n#### 2. **Stat Functions** (fileinfo.go)\n- `Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)` - Returns extended file metadata, following symbolic links\n- `Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)` - Returns extended file metadata, NOT following symbolic links\n\nBoth functions internally call fileInfoFromOS() which extracts data from syscall.Stat_t on Darwin.\n\n#### 3. **InstallSIGPIPEHandler** (sigpipe.go)\n```go\nfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n```\n- Installs a SIGPIPE signal handler that causes the process to exit with status 0\n- Uses signal.Notify with a buffered channel (not signal.Ignore)\n- Safe to call multiple times; each invocation starts one goroutine\n- Matches GNU coreutils behavior when stdout/stderr are closed by downstream consumers\n- Used by all cmd implementations (sponge, cat, version, true, false)\n\n#### 4. **Terminal Functions** (terminal.go)\n- `TerminalWidth() (int, error)` - Gets terminal width using TIOCGWINSZ ioctl\n- `IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool` - Checks if a file descriptor is a terminal\n\n#### 5. **Signal Handler for Terminal Resize** (signal.go)\n- `OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int))` - Registers callbacks for SIGWINCH signals\n  - Supports multiple callbacks in registration order\n  - Internally uses sync.Mutex and sync.Once for thread-safe callback management\n  - Handler calls TerminalWidth() on each SIGWINCH and invokes registered callbacks\n\n### Relevant Patterns in cmd/ Implementations\n\n#### Signal Handling Pattern\nAll cmd utilities follow a consistent pattern:\n```go\nfunc main() {\n    sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n    // ... rest of implementation\n    os.Exit(run())\n}\n```\n\nExamples:\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/cat/main.go` (lines 28-29)\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/sponge/main.go` (line 21)\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/version/main.go` (line 20)\n\n#### Atomic File Write Pattern (Sponge)\nThe sponge implementation demonstrates atomic file writing via temp-file rename:\n- Reads all stdin into memory or temp file (spillThreshold calculated from runtime.MemStats)\n- Uses os.CreateTemp() to create temp files with pattern \"sponge.XXXXXX\"\n- Temp directory determined by TMPDIR env var or \"/tmp\" fallback\n- Implements cleanup via defer for temp files\n- Full specification at: `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd007-sponge.yaml`\n\n#### Error Handling Pattern (Cat)\nThe cat implementation shows how to handle SIGPIPE explicitly:\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    if errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE) {\n        os.Exit(0)\n    }\n    fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"cat: %s\\n\", formatErr(err))\n    exitCode = 1\n}\n```\n\nThis allows for explicit handling of broken pipe errors before the signal handler catches them, demonstrating a defensive approach to SIGPIPE.\n\n### Design Notes\n\n1. **Platform-specific implementations** exist via build tags (e.g., `//go:build darwin` for stat_darwin.go)\n2. **Thread-safety** is emphasized in signal callbacks using sync.Mutex and sync.Once\n3. **Cleanup patterns** rely on Go's defer mechanism for resource cleanup\n4. **Specification-driven development** with reference binaries from GNU coreutils and moreutils"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67b0da97-d267-4c6d-8952-8e08eb6dda1c","uuid":"5cd88c0e-d54c-4e0e-881d-0b27205d015b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T02:53:43.536Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130, find and read the pkg/sys package files to understand what functions are exported, especially Lstat, FileInfo, and InstallSIGPIPEHandler. Also check if there are any other cmd/ implementations that do signal handling or atomic file writes that I could reference for patterns. Report the key exports from pkg/sys and any relevant patterns found.","agentId":"a1ebc2c9fa7d08227","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings:\n\n## Summary of pkg/sys Package Exports and Relevant Patterns\n\nBased on my exploration of the go-unix-utils repository, here are the key findings:\n\n### Key Exports from pkg/sys Package\n\n**File Location:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/pkg/sys/`\n\n#### 1. **FileInfo Struct** (fileinfo.go)\nA data structure holding extended file metadata unavailable through os.FileInfo:\n- `Mode` (os.FileMode)\n- `Size`, `Blocks`, `Blksize` (int64)\n- `Nlink`, `Uid`, `Gid`, `Rdev`, `Dev`, `Ino` (uint64 or uint32)\n- `ModTime`, `AccessTime`, `ChangeTime` (time.Time)\n- `Info` (os.FileInfo) - the underlying standard interface\n\n#### 2. **Stat Functions** (fileinfo.go)\n- `Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)` - Returns extended file metadata, following symbolic links\n- `Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error)` - Returns extended file metadata, NOT following symbolic links\n\nBoth functions internally call fileInfoFromOS() which extracts data from syscall.Stat_t on Darwin.\n\n#### 3. **InstallSIGPIPEHandler** (sigpipe.go)\n```go\nfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n```\n- Installs a SIGPIPE signal handler that causes the process to exit with status 0\n- Uses signal.Notify with a buffered channel (not signal.Ignore)\n- Safe to call multiple times; each invocation starts one goroutine\n- Matches GNU coreutils behavior when stdout/stderr are closed by downstream consumers\n- Used by all cmd implementations (sponge, cat, version, true, false)\n\n#### 4. **Terminal Functions** (terminal.go)\n- `TerminalWidth() (int, error)` - Gets terminal width using TIOCGWINSZ ioctl\n- `IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool` - Checks if a file descriptor is a terminal\n\n#### 5. **Signal Handler for Terminal Resize** (signal.go)\n- `OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int))` - Registers callbacks for SIGWINCH signals\n  - Supports multiple callbacks in registration order\n  - Internally uses sync.Mutex and sync.Once for thread-safe callback management\n  - Handler calls TerminalWidth() on each SIGWINCH and invokes registered callbacks\n\n### Relevant Patterns in cmd/ Implementations\n\n#### Signal Handling Pattern\nAll cmd utilities follow a consistent pattern:\n```go\nfunc main() {\n    sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n    // ... rest of implementation\n    os.Exit(run())\n}\n```\n\nExamples:\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/cat/main.go` (lines 28-29)\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/sponge/main.go` (line 21)\n- `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/cmd/version/main.go` (line 20)\n\n#### Atomic File Write Pattern (Sponge)\nThe sponge implementation demonstrates atomic file writing via temp-file rename:\n- Reads all stdin into memory or temp file (spillThreshold calculated from runtime.MemStats)\n- Uses os.CreateTemp() to create temp files with pattern \"sponge.XXXXXX\"\n- Temp directory determined by TMPDIR env var or \"/tmp\" fallback\n- Implements cleanup via defer for temp files\n- Full specification at: `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5130/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd007-sponge.yaml`\n\n#### Error Handling Pattern (Cat)\nThe cat implementation shows how to handle SIGPIPE explicitly:\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    if errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE) {\n        os.Exit(0)\n    }\n    fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"cat: %s\\n\", formatErr(err))\n    exitCode = 1\n}\n```\n\nThis allows for explicit handling of broken pipe errors before the signal handler catches them, demonstrating a defensive approach to SIGPIPE.\n\n### Design Notes\n\n1. **Platform-specific implementations** exist via build tags (e.g., `//go:build darwin` for stat_darwin.go)\n2. **Thread-safety** is emphasized in signal callbacks using sync.Mutex and sync.Once\n3. **Cleanup patterns** rely on Go's defer mechanism for resource cleanup\n4. **Specification-driven development** with reference binaries from GNU coreutils and moreutils"}],"totalDurationMs":46109,"totalTokens":40592,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_read_input_tokens":38346,"output_tokens":1291,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":950},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":5,"output_tokens":1291,"cache_read_input_tokens":38346,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":950,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":3,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
